A CHECKBOX NEVER LANDS ON A PARAGRAPH THAT HAD NONE.

Two nested lists split this item into THREE pieces: the first paragraph, the
middle one, and the tail. The author wrote ONE box, on the item. It belongs to
the piece that opens the item and to no other.

Hand it to every piece and the middle and the tail both come back as
`- [x] ...`. The document then asserts that two tasks are DONE which nobody
ever wrote as tasks, let alone ticked. That is worse than dropping a line. A
reader who loses a line can see something is missing. A reader handed an
invented finished task has no way to know it is false.

WHY THREE PIECES AND NOT TWO. One nested list gives a first piece and a tail,
and any rule that spends the box on the first piece passes that. It also
passes if the rule is "the box goes on the piece before the FIRST nested
list", which is a different rule and a wrong one. Two nested lists tell them
apart: the box must appear once and never again, however many times the item
is split.

THE SECOND CLAIM: only the first piece carries a marker. `Middle paragraph`
and `Tail paragraph` are continuations of the item, so they line up under its
content at two columns and carry no bullet. See
`format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item` for that defect on its own,
and `blocks-counts-an-item-split-by-a-nested-list-once` for what it did to the
item count.

The two claims are one invariant seen twice: a continuation has no marker, and
a checkbox is part of the marker. `ListItem.continuation` says which pieces
those are, and the parser spends both on the piece that emits first.
